  3. node -e 'import("<repo>/scripts/run-tests.mjs").then(m=>{const s=require("fs").readFileSync("/tmp/ic1pa-forge3.out","utf8").replace(/\x1b\[[0-9;]*[mGKH]/gu,"");console.log(m.areAllFailuresEnvironmental(s))})'
     -> true

Observed: true. In runParallel (scripts/run-tests.mjs:207-227) that means hasEnvironmentalMarker=true, failCount=1, allEnvironmental=true -> effectiveCode=75 and the phase prints 'REFUSED (environmental verdict, exit 75)' for a real product failure. fleet-check maps rc 75 to FAIL (bin/fleet-check:675), so the tree is not laundered, but the run's own log attributes a product red to the environment and any consumer that treats 75 as retryable re-runs it (the throughput cost this bead exists to remove).

Reachable for real: tests/quiet-window.test.ts:445-451 does exactly 'assertRejects(() => launchChrome({...}), QuietWindowRefusedError, "ENVIRONMENT:")'. If launchChrome throws any other error (a genuine regression of the refusal contract), the AssertionError message names the class and the classifier calls it environmental.

Expected: the delta's own round-1 P1 ruling was that a token in the TEST NAME must not mask an assertion failure; the same masking hazard remains for the token in the assertion BODY. A positive identification of an environmental refusal should require the refusal's own evidence (the ENVIRONMENT: message / the refusal error thrown uncaught), not any occurrence of the class token in the failure text.

Evidence: /tmp/ic1pa-forge3.out + /tmp/ic1pa-classify.mjs; scripts/run-tests.mjs:53-70 (errorBody markers added by this delta), 207-227; tests/quiet-window.test.ts:445-451; bin/fleet-check:675.
