Found during the independent review of chrome-agent-platform-0lb4 (2026-09-25), while checking whether the sites that bead's own text names were actually covered.

WHAT IS OBSERVED. scripts/security-suite.ts was NOT touched by 0lb4, and the bead text names it ("scripts/security-suite.ts's cdp.eval path plus its clickAt (:194-200, which does .catch(() => null)) are the same shape in a different file"). Measured against the current tree, one of those two named sites is already safe and one is live:

SAFE (so the bead's premise is half-stale - cdp.eval goes through openCdp() in scripts/lib/chrome-launch.ts:636, which throws on exceptionDetails; that was the mee3 repair). All `cdp.eval(...)` call sites in this file are therefore surfaced, not swallowed.

LIVE DEFECT 1 - a local raw evaluate that bypasses that helper: scripts/security-suite.ts:298-301
    const inWorld = async (expr) => {
      const r = await cdp.send("Runtime.evaluate", {...contextId: isolated.id...}, fx.sessionId);
      return r?.result?.result?.value;      // <-- no exceptionDetails check
    };
A page-side throw yields `undefined`. The consumer at :296 asserts `mainRuntime === "undefined" || mainRuntime === false` for "sender authority: a page's MAIN world has no chrome.runtime". So an INSTRUMENT failure is reported as a FAILED SENDER-AUTHORITY CHECK - a security-gate red that reads as a product/security defect. This is exactly the conversion 0lb4 exists to stop, still live in the gate whose composer:false the 0lb4 bead recounts.

LIVE DEFECT 2 - tolerance without a reason: scripts/security-suite.ts:199-200 `clickAt` does `cdp.eval(session, expr).catch(() => null)` then `if (!b || typeof b.x !== "number") return false`. Now that openCdp().eval THROWS, this catch swallows a real harness error into `false`, i.e. "the element was not clickable", with no comment saying why the tolerance is intended. 0lb4's FIX SHAPE item 3 requires tolerance be explicit at the call site WITH A COMMENT SAYING WHY; the catch is present, the why is not.

REPRODUCE. Drive scripts/security-suite.ts with a page expression that throws in the isolated-world path (e.g. temporarily break the selector feeding `centerOf(composerSend("hub"))`, or evaluate an expression that references a missing global) and observe that :296's sender-authority check reports a product failure rather than naming the evaluate that died; confirm clickAt returns false with no diagnostic distinguishing "no element" from "harness threw".

ACCEPTANCE. (1) inWorld inspects exceptionDetails and throws the page-side description (or is replaced by the shared helper, below) so an evaluate failure names itself instead of failing a security assertion; (2) clickAt keeps its tolerance but carries a one-line reason at the call site, and returns a NAMED diagnostic distinguishing "element not found" from "evaluate failed" - the payload shape 0lb4's FIX SHAPE item 4 prescribes ({error: "...: <selector>"}); (3) a falsification test that goes RED if either guard is removed, and that drives the real security-suite path rather than regex-asserting its source; (4) the sender-authority check must not be able to pass or fail on an undefined value - assert on a typed result.

CONSTRAINTS. Do not weaken any existing security assertion. The security custody chain is load-sensitive (canonical lock via CAP_SECURITY_NONCE) - classify any timeout there by measurement (scripts/lib/quiet-window.ts), never as a product red. See also chrome-agent-platform-kwrx for the shared-helper fix that would make the first defect un-omittable; this bead stands alone if that one is deferred.

Owner-of-record for the most load-sensitive gate in the repo: the 370-check Chrome journey suite (scripts/chrome-journeys.ts). chrome-agent-platform-35o1 found that this gate had NO owning issue — its beads (gsyp KEYLESS-FIRST-RESULT, xqhz the demo umbrella) closed and nothing noticed, so a lifecycle defect (9t1p) sat behind an unowned harness whose failures were classified environmental. This bead exists so the gate has an owner that outlives any individual fix.

WHAT THE OWNER DOES (all cheap, none of it a gate run):
- keeps the registry's `owner` field for this harness pointed at a LIVE bead (scripts/lib/harness-registry.ts; tests/harness-registry.test.ts fails when the named bead is closed);
- triages refusals and flakes: an exit 75 is environmental and needs a machine reading, never a retry-and-hope; a red that is not the tree's is filed, not waived;
- keeps the gate's evidence discoverable (the refusal payloads, the last green run, the version it was green at);
- hands over explicitly rather than closing silently: closing this bead REQUIRES re-pointing the registry field at a successor in the same change, which is the failure the guard exists to prevent.

NOT IN SCOPE: owning the fix for a defect the gate finds (that is the defect's own bead — 9t1p today), and the other gate-class harnesses (chrome-agent-platform-ryrr).

e273's scope was a DIRECTORY LIST (tests/ + scripts/), and the pattern spans more directories than the list named. Coord ruled on 2026-09-18 that the remaining set is a bead of its own, applying the k7c5 distinction: fileURLToPath for PATH-VALUED uses (roots, keys, labels, spawn arguments), leave a URL-relative use alone where it is genuinely a URL slice.

EIGHT INSTANCES, all tracked, all path-valued (verified on main 4c9231c9):
- cap-evidence/54k5-real-tree-variant.ts:13   const EXT = new URL("../extension", import.meta.url).pathname.replace(/\/$/, "");
- cap-evidence/acp-browser-acceptance.ts:17   const ROOT = new URL("..", import.meta.url).pathname;
- cap-evidence/h638-open-trace.ts:24           const ROOT = new URL("..", import.meta.url).pathname;
- cap-evidence/model-edit-still-refused-journey.ts:19  const ROOT = new URL("..", import.meta.url).pathname;
- cap-evidence/owner-edit-approval-journey.ts:25       const ROOT = new URL("..", import.meta.url).pathname;
- fixtures/webmcp-server.ts:21                 const ROOT = new URL(".", import.meta.url).pathname;
- packages/bundled/evidence/emscripten-abi/run-fixtures.mjs:101  new URL(import.meta.url).pathname (spawn argument)
- .agents/skills/impeccable/scripts/generate-image.mjs:274      spawnSync(process.execPath, [new URL('./embed-prompt.mjs', import.meta.url).pathname, ...])

WHY THE cap-evidence FIVE MATTER MOST: they are today's own verification harnesses — the instruments that produced the day's receipts — and they would fail on a spaced checkout, which is a legitimate environment. Fixing 'silently wrong on a spaced checkout' in the product while writing evidence scripts that do it is the pointed form of this defect.

META-LESSON FOR THE RECORD: define a sweep by the PATTERN and its uses, not by a directory list; a named-scope sweep leaves the pattern wherever the list did not reach. Same correction as 'sweep the whole set, not the named items', arriving from the test suite's side.

NOT BUGS, MUST STAY: tests/file-url-root-guard.test.ts carries the pattern as the thing it detects, and tests/substring-pin-honesty.test.ts has it inside a string literal (an analyzer fixture). A guard whose fixture trips a raw grep is the guard working; the detector masks code vs text.

ACCEPTANCE: each instance uses fileURLToPath where the value is a filesystem path; a spaced checkout drives the runnable ones (fixtures/webmcp-server.ts through its tests, run-fixtures.mjs if runnable) to their real behaviour; the existing guard is extended to the pattern's real scope (or the bead records why a directory cannot be scanned), with a falsification that a planted instance in a newly covered directory goes RED.

