description:
Reviewed chrome-agent-platform-l5rup delta 2125f3ff5..a59b0d1b7 (tip a59b0d1b7, branch fleet/qazo-l5rup).

Every branch of the \`if (this.inOsc)\` block (scripts/acp-bridge.ts:497-580) ends in \`continue\`,
so control can never reach the \`if (this.inEsc)\` block (scripts/acp-bridge.ts:582) with inOsc still
true. The new guard

  if (char === "]") {
    if (this.inOsc && this.inOscPendingAbort) {   // scripts/acp-bridge.ts:585 — unreachable
      this.flushUnterminatedOsc(onLine);
    } else {
      this.resetOsc();
    }
    ...
  }

therefore always takes the else branch; the 7-bit OSC-restart recovery it looks like it implements is
actually done in the inOscEsc handler at scripts/acp-bridge.ts:505-518. No behaviour is lost by
deleting the unreachable arm (or by asserting the invariant instead of repeating the condition);
static reasoning only — no runtime repro needed.

Evidence: scripts/acp-bridge.ts:497-580 (all-continue), :582-590, :505-518.
Fix owner: chrome-agent-platform-qazo (branch fleet/qazo-l5rup).

MEASURED while gating chrome-agent-platform-dsvq on this VM (2026-10-06). PRE-EXISTING and host-dependent — unrelated to any browser/resolution change.

WHAT: tests/durable-root.test.ts, 'isRamBacked identifies the tmpfs /tmp and disk-backed $HOME' asserts:
    assertEquals(isRamBacked("/tmp"), true, "/tmp is tmpfs on the build host");
    assertEquals(isRamBacked("/dev/shm"), true, "/dev/shm is tmpfs");
    assertEquals(isRamBacked(HOME ?? "/home"), false, "$HOME is disk");
On this VM /tmp is ext4 (findmnt: ext4), so the first assertion fails with 'AssertionError: Values are not equal: /tmp is tmpfs on the build host'. Measured in the dsvq worktree: 2 failures in the file — this one PLUS the offender guard — and the offender guard failure is separately owned (chrome-agent-platform-xvj4 repairs the three tmp offenders my fyvc file added). This one is NOT mine and not xvj4's: it is a test that reads ambient host state it does not create, which AGENTS' 'a suite must not depend on ambient state it does not create' (chrome-agent-platform-p15i) names as its own defect class.

WHY IT MATTERS: it means the durable-root guard cannot be green at all on any box with a disk-backed /tmp (container/VM images commonly mount /tmp on the root fs), and because the file is NOT in ALWAYS_ON (gap tracked as chrome-agent-platform-kz27) nobody notices: lanes see 'guard red, must be the offenders'. A red that can never be green trains lanes to ignore the file — the exact failure the kz27 gap already risks.

ACCEPTANCE: make the /tmp and /dev/shm expectations HOST-DERIVED (probe the filesystem type via findmnt/statfs and assert isRamBacked AGREES with the host, i.e. tmpfs→true / disk→false — the function's real contract is detection, not that /tmp is tmpfs), or provision the expectation explicitly. Keep /dev/shm asserted as tmpfs only where the host actually has it. Then the guard's red means offenders and nothing else, on every host.

FOUND while diagnosing chrome-agent-platform-s0fs (the 6-minute-budget hang). It is a SECOND, deterministic defect in the same harness and it is UPSTREAM of the hang: the harness now dies in ~3s at the collapsed-rail checks, so the hang cannot be re-verified live on this tree until this is settled.

MEASURED (2 runs out of 2, at origin/main 66752a71):
  FAIL: collapsed rail action buttons are uniformly 34×34 — {"newTask":{"w":36,"h":36},"newAgent":{"w":0,"h":0},"artifacts":{"w":36,"h":36},"directory":{"w":36,"h":36},"settings":{"w":36,"h":36}}
  FAIL: collapse control is a ≥44×44 nub hit target — {"w":36,"h":36}
  FAIL: nub straddles the sidebar edge + stays in-bounds — null
  FAIL: nub z-index is above the thread overlay — {"z":null,"tvz":100}

INDEPENDENT PROBE (own headless launch, fresh profile, NTP page, sampled at 2.5s AND 6.5s so a boot race is excluded):
  #side-toggle EXISTS at 28×28 (harness wants ≥44×44);
  #side-toggle .nub DOES NOT EXIST (harness reads it for the straddle + z-index checks, hence the nulls);
  rail action buttons measure 36×36 (harness demands 34×34; 716s.11's floor is ≥36px for primary controls).

TWO CANDIDATE RESOLUTIONS, and the choice is a product judgement, which is why this is filed rather than patched:
(a) the product deliberately changed (the nub replaced/renamed, rail actions grown to the 36px floor) → the harness must be re-truthed against the new markup, and its nub checks re-pointed or retired;
(b) the nub was meant to survive → this is a product regression (the collapse affordance lost its straddling hit target) and the harness is right.
Either way, until it is answered, ui-integration cannot complete, and its 6-minute budget question (the reason s0fs exists) cannot be measured.

FOLLOW-UP to chrome-agent-platform-ady6 (composer-selector migration audit, 2026-10-05).

cap-evidence/h638-open-trace.ts is STALE and cannot run. Its four selector constants are LIVE retired ids:

  line 155: const HUB_INPUT    = `document.getElementById("task-input")`;
  line 156: const HUB_SEND     = `document.getElementById("run-task")`;
  line 157: const THREAD_INPUT = `document.getElementById("thread-composer")?.querySelector("#task-input")`;
  line 158: const THREAD_SEND  = `document.getElementById("thread-composer")?.querySelector("#run-task")`;

They are used for typeInto/clickSel at :173,:174,:181,:182,:204,:205. Those ids were removed on purpose (bead sndb), so every one of those calls now finds nothing and the harness exits 1 ("hub composer not found") before measuring any latency at all.

FIX: import composerInput/composerSend from ../scripts/lib/composer-target.ts and use composerInput("hub")/composerSend("hub")/composerInput("thread")/composerSend("thread").

WHY NOT BUNDLED INTO ady6: repairing it is four lines, but the file's contract is "MEASURE task-open latency … re-run after any change", so proving the repair needs a REAL latency run (developer features flag, seeded history, in-flight run) — a mechanical diff plus the guard would not be evidence that it measures anything. The other cap-evidence driver in the ledger (sndb-composer-unique-evidence.ts) is the opposite case: it is a negative-assertion audit and MUST keep naming the ids; ady6 annotated that in the ledger.

comments.text:
CORRECTION AND EVIDENCE — the author (cap-ds-flash) retracts the "environmental" attribution it first reported to coord for this failure. A reviewer (cap-astra) correctly objected that "passes alone + full suite green" does not establish cause, and asked for preserved logs, exact command/base/HEAD, the selected set, and a pristine-base reproduction. Here is that work; the failure remains UNATTRIBUTED.

WHAT ACTUALLY RAN, established by preserving and reading the log (`~/cap-evidence/4lc0/subset-failure/changed-fail-webmcp.log`, 6062 lines): the command was `npm run test:changed` at the 4lc0 revise-3 tree, and it was **not a subset at all** — `select-tests: FAIL CLOSED — changed file(s) with no reachable test cannot be proved covered by a subset: extension/manifest.json, package-lock.json, package.json` → **"Running the FULL suite (npm test) instead."** Those three are build-rewritten by the version bump, not by the detector change. So the red occurred in a full-suite run whose serial phase the fallback re-ran (build-bootstrap et al.), which is a different file set from the `npm test` run I compared it against — the comparison I used was therefore not like-for-like.

THE FAILING ASSERTION AND THE MECHANISM HYPOTHESIS IT SUGGESTS: `assertEquals(detections.length, 1, "nonce-less page forgery must not update the registry")` (tests/webmcp-detect-auth.test.ts:86), failing as actual 0 / expected 1. Three lines above it, the test posts a message and waits a FIXED 100 ms (`await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 100))`) before asserting the authenticated probe was relayed. A late relay under load would produce exactly this 0-vs-1. **That is a hypothesis consistent with the observation, not a demonstration.**

REPRODUCTION ATTEMPTS, all negative (10 runs, logs beside the failure log):
- tip `2fd24126`, three heavyweight files in the same invocation: 3/3 PASS;
- pristine base `origin/main` (a separate worktree, none of the 4lc0 changes), same invocation: 3/3 PASS;
- tip under concurrent self-load (a 60-file `deno test` invocation of my own running in parallel): 2/2 PASS;
- pristine base under the same concurrent self-load: 2/2 PASS.
No manufactured load was applied to the box — only my own test invocations — so no other lane was affected.

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH. It does NOT establish that the 4lc0 change is innocent: the base passing shows the base *can* pass, not that the change is unrelated. It does NOT establish the 100 ms hypothesis. It DOES establish that the failure is rare enough that neither tree reds in ten attempts, and that my original "environmental" wording was a conclusion drawn from correlation — retracted here. The red stays recorded beside the green, unattributed, exactly as the reviewer asked: this bead tracks it, it does not attribute it.

